[' Here's a JavaScript implementation using async/await with Node.js-style streams and Promises, integrated with Winston for logging. This example demonstrates a scalable data collection function that manages multiple concurrent streams, handles random delays, supports pausing and resuming, and ensures data integrity.

```javascript
const stream = require('stream');
const winston = require('winston');

class DataCollector extends stream.Transform {
  constructor(options) {
    super({ ...options, objectMode: true });
    this._paused = false;
    this._sources = [];
    this._pendingData = [];
  }

  _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
    if (this._paused) {
      this._pendingData.push(chunk);
      return callback();
    }

    this._processData(chunk);
    callback();
  }

  async _processData(data) {
    try {
      for (const source of this._sources) {
        await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
          source.push(data, (err, data) => {
            if (err) return reject(err);
            if (!data) return resolve();
            this._handleData(data);
            resolve();
          });
        });
      }
    } catch (error) {
      winston.error(`Error processing data: ${error.message}`);
    }
  }

  _handleData(data) {
    // Process the data here, e.g., store it in a database or perform further transformations
  }

  pause() {
    this._paused = true;
  }

  resume() {
    this._paused = false;
    if (this._pendingData.length > 0) {
      this._processData(this._pendingData.pop());
    }
  }

  addSource(source) {
    this._sources.push(source);
  }
}

// Example usage:
const collector = new DataCollector({ highWaterMark: 10 });
collector.on('data', (chunk) => winston.info(`Received data: ${JSON.stringify(chunk)}`));
collector.on('error', (err) => winston.error(`Error: ${err.message}`));

// Add external APIs as streams to the collector
const api1 = new stream.Readable({ read() {} });
api1._read = () => {
  // Simulate API delay with a random time between 50ms and 200ms
  const delay = Math.random() * 150 + 50;
  setTimeout(() => api1.push(JSON.parse('{"data": "api1_data"})'), delay);
};

const api2 = new stream.Readable({ read() {} });
api2._read = () => {
  // Simulate API delay with a random time between 100ms and 300ms
  const delay = Math.random() * 200 + 100;
  setTimeout(() => api2.push(JSON.parse('{"data": "api2_data"})'), delay);
};

collector.addSource(api1);
collector.addSource(api2);
```

This code creates a custom `DataCollector` stream that manages multiple concurrent streams, handles pausing and resuming, and logs status updates and errors using Winston. The example demonstrates how to add new data sources by creating streams and pushing data into the collector.']

[" Here's a JavaScript utility function named `findElementsBySelector` that meets the specified requirements:

```javascript
class VisitedNodesMap {
  constructor() {
    this.map = new Map();
  }

  has(node) {
    return this.map.has(node);
  }

  set(node, selector) {
    this.map.set(node, selector);
  }
}

function findElementsBySelector(root, selector, depthLimit = Infinity, visitedNodesMap = new VisitedNodesMap()) {
  if (depthLimit <= 0 || visitedNodesMap.has(root)) return [];

  const results = [];
  let currentDepth = 1;

  const traverse = (node) => {
    if (!node || node.nodeType !== Node.ELEMENT_NODE) return;

    if (visitedNodesMap.has(node)) return;

    visitedNodesMap.set(node, selector);

    if (currentDepth > depthLimit) return;

    if (node.matches && node.matches(selector)) {
      results.push(node);
    }

    Array.from(node.children).forEach((childNode) => traverse(childNode));
  };

  traverse(root);
  return results;
}
```

This implementation uses a `VisitedNodesMap` class to keep track of visited nodes and prevent re-traversal. The main function `findElementsBySelector` takes a root node, selector, depth limit, and a visited nodes map as parameters. It traverses the DOM tree efficiently while respecting the provided depth limit and avoiding re-visiting nodes.

The function handles nested structures and non-element nodes gracefully by checking if a node is an element before adding it to the visited nodes map and performing further processing. It also accounts for changes occurring during traversal, as the visited nodes map ensures that once a node has been processed, it won't be re-traversed.

The function is optimized for both memory usage and execution speed by using a Map data structure to store visited nodes and minimizing the number of recursive calls through early returns when possible. The solution is robust against potential performance bottlenecks as it efficiently handles large trees or deep nesting.

Developers can balance between the breadth of the search and the depth of traversal by configuring the `depthLimit` parameter. The function integrates seamlessly with existing codebases, as it accepts a custom visited nodes map instance to allow for integration with any existing node tracking mechanism."]

