[' Here's a basic outline for the C# class you requested. I've used `ConcurrentDictionary` for efficient concurrent access, `MemoryStream` for handling streams, and `HMACSHA256` and `TripleDESCryptoServiceProvider` for encryption/decryption and HMAC validation respectively.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;

public class SecureMessageProcessor
{
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> _decryptedMessages = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, string>();
    private readonly HMACSHA256 _hmac;
    private readonly TripleDESCryptoServiceProvider _tripleDES;
    private readonly IConfiguration _configuration;

    public SecureMessageProcessor(IConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _configuration = configuration;
        var key = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(_configuration["EncryptionKey"]);
        var iv = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(_configuration["InitializationVector"]);
        _hmac = new HMACSHA256(key);
        _tripleDES = new TripleDESCryptoServiceProvider { Key = key, IV = iv };
    }

    public async Task<string> ProcessMessageAsync(string base64EncodedMessage)
    {
        if (!ValidateMessageIntegrity(base64EncodedMessage)) return null;

        var messageBytes = Convert.FromBase64String(base64EncodedMessage);
        using (var memoryStream = new MemoryStream(messageBytes))
        {
            using (var cryptoStream = new CryptoStream(memoryStream, _tripleDES.CreateDecryptor(), CryptoStreamMode.Read))
            {
                var decryptedBytes = new byte[messageBytes.Length];
                await cryptoStream.ReadAsync(decryptedBytes, 0, decryptedBytes.Length);
                return Encoding.UTF8.GetString(decryptedBytes);
            }
        }
    }

    private bool ValidateMessageIntegrity(string base64EncodedMessage)
    {
        var messageBytes = Convert.FromBase64String(base64EncodedMessage);
        var hmacBytes = _hmac.ComputeHash(messageBytes);
        var combined = new byte[messageBytes.Length + hmacBytes.Length];
        Buffer.BlockCopy(messageBytes, 0, combined, 0, messageBytes.Length);
        Buffer.BlockCopy(hmacBytes, 0, combined, messageBytes.Length, hmacBytes.Length);

        var combinedBase64 = Convert.ToBase64String(combined);
        return combinedBase64.EndsWith(_configuration["HMACSignature"]);
    }
}
```

This code does not include caching or resource management, as those aspects would require a more complex design and are beyond the scope of this example. You can implement caching using `ConcurrentDictionary`'s `TryAdd` method with a custom policy that removes least recently used items when the cache exceeds a certain size. For resource management, you could monitor memory usage and CPU consumption using .NET's built-in profiling tools or third-party libraries.']

[' In this example, I'll provide a C# function using the `System.Security.Cryptography` namespace for MD5 hashing and integrate it into a hypothetical logging framework. To minimize memory usage and optimize performance, we'll use streaming APIs for both hashing and logging. For concurrent execution from multiple threads, I'll utilize the `Task` class and `lock` statement to ensure thread safety.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public static async Task LogMD5HashAsync(string input, Action<string> logToCentralStorage)
{
    var md5 = MD5.Create();
    using (var inputStream = new MemoryStream(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input)))
    {
        using (var hashStream = new MemoryStream())
        {
            await md5.TransformStreamAsync(inputStream, hashStream);
            var hashedData = hashStream.ToArray();

            // Convert the byte array to a hexadecimal string for logging
            var hashedString = BitConverter.ToString(hashedData).Replace("-", "").ToLowerInvariant();

            lock (logSyncRoot)
            {
                logToCentralStorage(hashedString);
            }
        }
    }
}

private static readonly object logSyncRoot = new object();
```

This function takes an input string and a `Action<string>` delegate for logging to the central storage system. It creates an MD5 hash of the input string using streaming APIs, converts the resulting byte array into a hexadecimal string, and logs it using the provided action. The `lock` statement ensures thread safety when logging the hashed output.

To integrate with various external logging services, you can modify the `logToCentralStorage` delegate to use different APIs or adapters for each service. This design allows for easy integration of new logging services while maintaining data integrity and efficient transmission of logs.

For high-throughput scenarios, consider using a producer-consumer pattern or other concurrent collection classes like `BlockingCollection<T>` to manage the log entries efficiently. Additionally, you can implement caching strategies to reduce the number of hash calculations when processing duplicate inputs.']

