[" I'm providing a simplified C++ example using std::vector for dynamic arrays and std::mutex for thread safety. This code is not optimized for performance or memory usage in a production environment but serves as a starting point for your implementation.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <iostream>

struct RRC_MESSAGE { /* Define RRC_MESSAGE structure here */ };

struct NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_T {
    uint32_t UE_ID;
    uint8_t SFI;
    uint8_t UL_CCCH_MESSAGE_TYPE;
    std::vector<RRC_MESSAGE> rrc_messages;
    std::mutex mutex;
};

class NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_POOL {
private:
    std::vector<NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_T> pool;
    uint32_t next_id = 0;
    std::mutex pool_mutex;

public:
    NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_POOL(uint32_t size) {
        pool.reserve(size);
        for (auto& msg : pool) {
            msg.UE_ID = next_id++;
            msg.rrc_messages.clear();
            msg.mutex = std::mutex{};
        }
    }

    NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_T* acquire() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(pool_mutex);
        if (!pool.empty()) {
            auto it = pool.begin();
            pool.erase(it);
            return &(*it);
        }
        return nullptr;
    }

    void release(NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_T* msg) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(msg->mutex);
        pool.push_back(*msg);
    }
};
```

This code defines a `NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_POOL` class that manages a pool of `NFAPI_UL_CCCH_MSG_T` objects, each with its own vector of `RRC_MESSAGE` structures. The pool is thread-safe using std::mutex for locking when acquiring and releasing messages.

The constructor initializes the pool with a given size, assigns unique UE_IDs to each message, and clears the RRC_MESSAGES vector in each structure. The `acquire()` function retrieves an available message from the pool, and the `release()` function returns a message back to the pool for reuse.

You can further optimize this code by implementing custom memory allocation strategies, using more efficient data structures, or fine-tuning locking mechanisms based on your specific use case and system requirements."]

