[' Here's a simplified version of the requested JavaScript function. This example uses React for building the navigation bar, which provides a more efficient and scalable solution for handling large amounts of data (up to 1000 links in this case). It also includes basic conditional rendering based on user roles, dependency handling, and third-party analytics integration.

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';
import Analytics from 'react-ga';

const NavLink = ({ href, text, icon, isVisible, onClick, analyticsEvent }) => {
  if (!isVisible) return null;

  const handleClick = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    if (onClick) onClick(event);
    if (analyticsEvent) Analytics.event({ eventCategory: 'Navigation', eventAction: analyticsEvent });
  };

  return (
    <li className="nav-item">
      {icon && <span className={`nav-icon ${icon}`} />}
      <a href={href} className="nav-link" onClick={handleClick}>
        {text}
      </a>
    </li>
  );
};

NavLink.propTypes = {
  href: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
  text: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
  icon: PropTypes.string,
  isVisible: PropTypes.bool.isRequired,
  onClick: PropTypes.func,
  analyticsEvent: PropTypes.string,
};

const Navbar = ({ links, userRole, dependencies }) => {
  const navLinks = links.filter((link) => link.isVisible(userRole, dependencies));

  return (
    <ul className="navbar-nav">
      {navLinks.map((link) => (
        <NavLink key={link.href} {...link} />
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
};

Navbar.propTypes = {
  links: PropTypes.arrayOf(
    PropTypes.shape({
      href: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
      text: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
      icon: PropTypes.string,
      isVisible: PropTypes.func.isRequired,
    })
  ).isRequired,
  userRole: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
  dependencies: PropTypes.object,
};

export default Navbar;
```

In this example, the `NavLink` component represents each navigation link with properties for name, URL, icon, visibility, click event handler, and analytics event. The `Navbar` component takes an array of links, user role, and dependencies as props to filter visible links based on user permissions and system state.

The `isVisible` function in the link configuration object is responsible for determining whether a specific link should be displayed or hidden based on user roles and dependencies. This allows for flexible conditional rendering of navigation links.

To handle external data sources, you can modify the `dependencies` prop to include any required data and update the `isVisible` function accordingly.

The third-party analytics service is integrated using the `react-ga` library, which tracks navigation events when a link is clicked. To ensure performance under high traffic conditions, the analytics integration is asynchronous and only executed when necessary (i.e., when a link is clicked).']

