[' Here's a simplified C++ example of how you might structure this function. Note that this is a high-level implementation and may require adjustments based on specific system dependencies, libraries, and standards.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <memory>

struct RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG {
    uint32_t rrc_cgi;
    uint16_t rnti;
    uint8_t srb1_rlc_status;
};

struct NFAPI_L2_RXUEINFO_PDU {
    std::vector<RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG> rx_ues;
    std::mutex mutex;
};

std::vector<std::unique_ptr<NFAPI_L2_RXUEINFO_PDU>> buffer;
std::mutex buffer_mutex;
std::mutex log_mutex;

void log(const char* message) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(log_mutex);
    std::cout << message << std::endl;
}

void handle_ue_data(const RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG& ue_data) {
    // Allocate a new buffer if necessary
    if (buffer.size() >= buffer.capacity()) {
        log("Resizing buffer...");
        buffer.resize(buffer.capacity() * 2);
    }

    auto it = std::find_if(buffer.begin(), buffer.end(), [](const auto& p) { return p->rx_ues.empty(); });

    if (it != buffer.end()) {
        // Found an empty buffer, use it
        it->get()->mutex.lock();
        it->get()->rx_ues.push_back(ue_data);
        it->get()->mutex.unlock();
    } else {
        // No empty buffer found, allocate a new one and add the data
        auto new_buffer = std::make_unique<NFAPI_L2_RXUEINFO_PDU>();
        new_buffer->rx_ues.push_back(ue_data);
        buffer.push_back(std::move(new_buffer));
    }
}

void update_ue_data(uint16_t rnti, const RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG& new_data) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(buffer_mutex);

    for (auto& buffer : buffer) {
        buffer->mutex.lock();
        for (auto it = buffer->rx_ues.begin(); it != buffer->rx_ues.end(); ++it) {
            if (it->rnti == rnti) {
                it->rrc_cgi = new_data.rrc_cgi;
                it->srb1_rlc_status = new_data.srb1_rlc_status;
                buffer->mutex.unlock();
                return;
            }
        }
        buffer->mutex.unlock();
    }
}
```

This code creates a `NFAPI_L2_RXUEINFO_PDU` structure to hold the UE information and a vector of unique pointers to these structures. The `handle_ue_data` function manages memory allocation, finding an empty buffer if possible or creating a new one when necessary. The `update_ue_data` function allows for partial updates by iterating through all buffers and updating the specified RNTI with the new data.

The log function is used to track successful allocations and any failures during the process. This example uses mutexes to ensure thread safety, which helps handle concurrent requests and temporary outages from external data sources. The buffer can be resized dynamically to accommodate increasing data volumes.']

[' Here's a C function that uses the MD5 hash algorithm to generate a unique file identifier for a given filename. The function handles filenames with spaces, special characters, and null characters, ensuring the total length of the new filename does not exceed 255 characters.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <openssl/md5.h>

#define DEFAULT_DIGIT_LENGTH 10
#define MAX_FILENAME_LENGTH 256

char* generate_identifier(const char* filename) {
    if (!filename || strlen(filename) >= MAX_FILENAME_LENGTH)
        return NULL;

    unsigned char digest[MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH];
    MD5_CTX ctx;
    MD5_Init(&ctx);
    MD5_Update(&ctx, filename, strlen(filename));
    MD5_Final(digest, &ctx);

    char identifier[MAX_FILENAME_LENGTH];
    snprintf(identifier, sizeof(identifier), "%s_%0*x", filename, DEFAULT_DIGIT_LENGTH * 2, digest);

    // Trim the identifier if it exceeds the maximum length
    if (strlen(identifier) > MAX_FILENAME_LENGTH - strlen(filename) - 1) {
        identifier[MAX_FILENAME_LENGTH - strlen(filename) - 1] = '\0';
    }

    return identifier;
}
```

This function uses the OpenSSL library to perform the MD5 hash calculation. The resulting hash is converted into a hexadecimal string and appended to the original filename, separated by an underscore (_). If the filename is empty or contains only non-alphabetic characters, a default sequence of 10 digits is appended instead.

The function ensures that the total length of the new filename does not exceed 255 characters, including the null terminator. It also handles filenames with spaces, special characters, and null characters appropriately.']

