constexpr size_t DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 10;
constexpr char PRINTABLE_CHARS[] = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789!@#$%^&*()_+-=[]{}|;:,.<>?/";
constexpr size_t MAX_KEY_LENGTH = 10;
constexpr size_t MAX_STRING_LENGTH = 50;
constexpr size_t MAX_MEMORY_USAGE = 1024 * 1024 * 10; // 10 MB

struct Record {
    long offset;
    int key;
    std::string value;
};

class RecordPool {
public:
    RecordPool(size_t recordType, size_t initialCapacity = DEFAULT_CAPACITY)
        : m_recordTypes{recordType}, m_offsets{}, m_keys{}, m_records{}, m_memoryUsage(0), m_mutex() {
        reserve(initialCapacity);
    }

    Record* allocateRecord() {
        if (m_freeRecords.empty()) {
            auto newRecord = std::make_unique<Record>();
            new (newRecord.get()) Record();
            initializeRecord(*newRecord, m_recordTypes);
            m_records.push_back(std::move(newRecord));
        }
        auto record = m_freeRecords.back().release();
        m_freeRecords.pop_back();
        return record;
    }

    void freeRecord(Record* record) {
        record->~Record();
        m_freeRecords.push_back(std::unique_ptr<Record>(record));
    }

    Record& getRecord(size_t index) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_mutex);
        if (index >= m_records.size()) {
            reserve(index + 1);
        }
        return m_records[index];
    }

    void reserve(size_t newCapacity) {
        if (newCapacity > m_records.capacity()) {
            auto oldRecords = std::move(m_records);
            m_records.reserve(newCapacity);
            for (auto& record : oldRecords) {
                m_records.push_back(std::move(record));
            }
        }
    }

    void compact() {
        std::vector<Record> newRecords;
        std::unordered_map<long, size_t> offsetMap;
        std::unordered_map<int, size_t> keyMap;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < m_records.size(); ++i) {
            auto& record = m_records[i];
            if (!record.offset || !record.key) continue;
            offsetMap[record.offset] = newRecords.size();
            keyMap[record.key] = newRecords.size();
            newRecords.push_back(record);
        }
        m_records.swap(newRecords);
        for (auto& pair : offsetMap) {
            m_offsets[pair.second] = pair.first;
        }
        for (auto& pair : keyMap) {
            m_keys[pair.second] = pair.first;
        }
    }

private:
    void initializeRecord(Record& record, size_t recordType) {
        auto keyLength = MAX_KEY_LENGTH + std::to_string(recordType).length();
        auto stringLength = MAX_STRING_LENGTH + keyLength;
        record.value.resize(stringLength);
        generateRandomString(record.value.data(), stringLength);
        record.offset = m_records.size();
        record.key = m_keys.size() + 1;
    }

    void generateRandomString(char* str, size_t length) {
        std::srand(std::time(nullptr));
        for (size_t i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
            str[i] = PRINTABLE_CHARS[std::rand() % sizeof(PRINTABLE_CHARS) - 1];
        }
    }

    size_t m_recordTypes;
    std::vector<long> m_offsets;
    std::vector<int> m_keys;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Record>> m_records;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Record>> m_freeRecords;
    size_t m_memoryUsage;
    std::mutex m_mutex;
};
```

This implementation uses a `RecordPool` class to manage the collection of records. The pool maintains a vector of unique pointers to `Record` objects, with a separate vector for free records that can be reused when needed. The `Record` structure contains an offset, key, and dynamically allocated string.

The `initializeRecord()` function initializes a record with a random value, random string, and sets the offset and key fields accordingly. The `generateRandomString()` function generates a random string using printable ASCII characters.

The pool also includes methods for allocating and freeing records, as well as compacting the data structure to optimize memory usage. The `compact()` method rebuilds the offset and key arrays to minimize fragmentation.

To handle dynamic resizing of the key and offset arrays, the pool uses a vector that automatically grows as needed when new records are added. When the capacity is exceeded during a batch operation, the pool calls the `reserve()` function to increase its capacity.

The pool also includes a mechanism for periodically checking and cleaning up unused memory by maintaining a free list of records that have been freed but not yet reused.

To ensure thread safety, the pool uses a mutex to protect access to the internal data structures during concurrent access. The performance implications of synchronization mechanisms will depend on the specific use case and number of threads accessing the pool simultaneously.

The function can handle a variable number of record types by accepting a `recordType` parameter in the constructor, which is used to determine the key length and maximum string size for each record type. The pool uses a vector of unique pointers to Records, allowing it to support different record types with varying sizes.

To enforce the maximum memory usage limit, the pool maintains a `m_memoryUsage` variable that tracks the current memory usage. When adding a new record, if the total memory usage exceeds the specified limit, the pool will compact the data structure to free up some memory before allocating a new record.

To minimize the frequency of reallocations and memory fragmentation, the pool uses a combination of dynamic arrays (vectors) and a free list for recycling freed records. The `compact()` method is called periodically to further optimize memory usage.

Finally, the function can be integrated into an existing system by calling its constructor when needed and using the `getRecord()`, `allocateRecord()`, and `freeRecord()` methods as appropriate. To monitor its memory usage and performance impact on the overall system, you can track the `m_memoryUsage` variable and profile the function's execution time.

