[' In this example, I'll provide a simplified version of the `selectAttack` function using C++17 and the `<thread>`, `<mutex>`, and `<fstream>` standard libraries. This solution uses global variables for cooldown tracking and log management, but it's important to note that a more production-ready implementation would likely use a centralized data store or a database for better scalability and concurrency control.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <chrono>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <map>

// Global variables for cooldown tracking and log management
std::mutex mtx;
std::map<int, std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point> cooldowns;
std::vector<std::string> logs;
constexpr int NUM_ATTACKS = 4;
constexpr int MIN_COOLDOWN = 8;
constexpr int MAX_COOLDOWN = 10;
const std::string attackNames[NUM_ATTACKS] = {"Attack1", "Attack2", "Attack3", "Attack4"};
const std::string logFormat = "%Y - %m/%d/%y - %H:%M:%S - Character Attacked with: %s\n";

void log(const std::string& message) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    logs.push_back(message);
}

bool canAttack(int attackId) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    auto it = cooldowns.find(attackId);
    if (it != cooldowns.end()) {
        auto elapsed = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - it->second;
        if (elapsed > std::chrono::seconds(MIN_COOLDOWN) && elapsed < std::chrono::seconds(MAX_COOLDOWN)) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    cooldowns[attackId] = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    return true;
}

void selectAttack() {
    int attackId = rand() % NUM_ATTACKS;
    if (canAttack(attackId)) {
        log(std::string(logFormat, std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(std::chrono::steady_clock::now()), attackNames[attackId]));
    } else {
        std::cout << "Attack on cooldown. Attempted to use: " << attackNames[attackId] << '\n';
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        threads.push_back(std::thread(selectAttack));
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }

    std::ofstream logFile("attack_log.txt", std::ios::app);
    for (const auto& logEntry : logs) {
        logFile << logEntry;
    }
    logFile.close();

    return 0;
}
```

This code demonstrates a simple implementation of the `selectAttack` function with cooldown tracking, logging, and concurrent execution using threads. The global variables are used to maintain the attack cooldown and log management, while the `log` function is provided for logging attack selections and usage statistics. The main function creates multiple threads that execute the `selectAttack` function concurrently, and logs the results to a file named "attack_log.txt" after all threads have completed their execution.']

