[' Here's a high-level outline of how you might structure this Rust function using some popular libraries for HTTP requests (`reqwest`), logging (`log`), and serialization (`serde`). Note that due to the complexity of the task, I'll provide an overview of the main components rather than writing the entire code.

```rust
use reqwest::Client;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use log::{debug, error};
use serde_json::Value;
use url::Url;

type FactoryId = String;
type DataSourceAdapter = Box<dyn Fn(Url) -> Result<Value, reqwest::Error>>;

struct Registry {
    adapters: HashMap<FactoryId, DataSourceAdapter>,
}

impl Registry {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Registry {
            adapters: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    fn register(&mut self, id: FactoryId, adapter: DataSourceAdapter) {
        self.adapters.insert(id, adapter);
    }
}

struct HttpClient {
    client: Client,
    registry: Registry,
    validate_certificate: bool,
}

impl HttpClient {
    fn new(registry: Registry, validate_certificate: bool) -> Self {
        let client = Client::builder()
            .danger_accept_invalid_certs(!validate_certificate)
            .build()
            .expect("Failed to create HTTP client");

        HttpClient {
            client,
            registry,
            validate_certificate,
        }
    }

    fn request_builder(&self, url: Url) -> Result<reqwest::blocking::RequestBuilder, reqwest::Error> {
        let adapter = self.registry.adapters.get(&url.host_str().unwrap().to_string()).cloned();
        match adapter {
            Some(adapter) => Ok(self.client.request(url)),
            None => Err(reqwest::Error::new(reqwest::ErrorKind::Other, "No data source adapter found")),
        }
    }

    fn execute_request(&self, request: reqwest::blocking::RequestBuilder) -> Result<Value, reqwest::Error> {
        let response = request.send()?;
        if !response.is_success() {
            error!("HTTP request failed with status code {}", response.status());
            return Err(reqwest::Error::new(reqwest::ErrorKind::ClientError, "HTTP request failed"));
        }

        let body = response.text()?;
        Ok(serde_json::from_str(&body)?)
    }
}

fn createHttpClientWithDynamicDependencies(registry: Registry, validate_certificate: bool) -> HttpClient {
    HttpClient::new(registry, validate_certificate)
}
```

This code provides a basic structure for the `createHttpClientWithDynamicDependencies` function. It creates an HTTP client with dynamic dependencies using a registry to manage different data source adapters. The client can handle requests and responses efficiently while logging errors and providing insights into system performance. However, you'll need to implement the actual data source adapters and error handling for various failure scenarios.']

