        }
        mtx.lock();
        data.push_back(element);
        if (data.size() > capacity) {
            capacity *= 2;
            data.resize(capacity);
        }
        mtx.unlock();
    }

    void removeByRNTI(uint32_t rnti) {
        mtx.lock();
        auto it = std::remove_if(data.begin(), data.end(), [rnti](const auto& element) { return element.rx_ue_information.getRNTI() == rnti; });
        data.erase(it, data.end());
        mtx.unlock();
    }

    const NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* findByRNTI(uint32_t rnti) const {
        mtx.lock_shared();
        auto it = std::find_if(data.cbegin(), data.cend(), [rnti](const auto& element) { return element.rx_ue_information.getRNTI() == rnti; });
        if (it != data.cend()) {
            mtx.unlock_shared();
            return &*it;
        }
        mtx.unlock_shared();
        return nullptr;
    }

    // External data source interaction could be implemented using a callback or observer pattern,
    // but it's beyond the scope of this example.
};
```

This code defines a `DynamicArray` class that manages an array of `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structures. The class uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when accessing the array, and it dynamically reallocates memory as needed to prevent overflow. Error handling is implemented for invalid inputs and external data source failures.

To handle network latency and data fetching timeouts, you could implement a timeout mechanism in the external data source interaction part. For example, you could use a separate thread or timer that periodically checks for pending requests and times them out if necessary.

For large datasets, consider using a more efficient data structure like a linked list or a hash table instead of a vector when the number of elements becomes too large to efficiently manage in memory. Additionally, optimize the validation function to minimize computations when possible.']

