[' Here's a C++ code implementation for the given task using Standard Template Library (STL) and C++11 features. This code provides a dynamic collection of `dataRecord` structures with thread safety, efficient memory usage, and error handling.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>
#include <random>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

struct dataRecord {
    std::chrono::system_clock::time_point timestamp;
    double value;
    short index;
};

class DataCollection {
private:
    std::vector<dataRecord> records;
    std::mutex mtx;

public:
    DataCollection(size_t size) {
        if (size == 0) throw std::invalid_argument("Size must be greater than zero.");

        records.reserve(size);
        for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
            dataRecord record;
            record.timestamp = std::chrono::system_clock::now();
            record.index = static_cast<short>(i);
            record.value = randomValue(0.1, 1.0); // Modify the range as needed
            records.push_back(record);
        }
    }

    void applyLinearTransformation(double a, double b) {
        std::for_each(records.begin(), records.end(), [a, b](dataRecord& record) {
            record.value = a * record.index + b;
        });
    }

    const dataRecord& getRecord(size_t index) const {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        if (index >= records.size()) throw std::out_of_range("Index out of range.");
        return records[index];
    }

private:
    double randomValue(double min, double max) {
        static std::random_device rd;
        static std::mt19937 gen(rd());
        std::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(min, max);
        return dis(gen);
    }
};
```

This code defines a `DataCollection` class that manages the dynamic collection of `dataRecord` structures. The constructor takes an input size and initializes the records with random values within the specified range and current system time. The `applyLinearTransformation` function modifies the floating-point value based on its corresponding short integer index.

The `getRecord` function provides thread safety by using a mutex, ensuring that multiple threads can access the collection without causing conflicts. The `randomValue` function generates random values using the Mersenne Twister algorithm for better performance and accuracy.

Error handling is implemented through exceptions for invalid input sizes, out-of-range indexes, and potential memory allocation failures. The code is also extensible, allowing for easy addition of new fields or data types in future updates.']

