            data[i].tag.value = i; // Replace this with your actual RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG implementation
            data[i].rnti = rand() % UINT32_MAX;
            data[i].crcIndication.crcFlag = rand() % 2;
        }
        std::clock_t end = std::clock();
        double elapsedTime = static_cast<double>(end - start) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC * 1000;
        log_message(std::string("Allocated and populated ") + std::to_string(numElements) + " elements in " + std::to_string(elapsedTime) + " ms");
    }

    void deallocate() {
        if (capacity > 0) {
            munmap(const_cast<char*>(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(data.data())), capacity * sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
            data.clear();
            capacity = 0;
        }
    }
};

void* worker(void* arg) {
    CRCIndicationAllocator* allocator = static_cast<CRCIndicationAllocator*>(arg);
    size_t numElements = rand() % 10000 + 1; // Adjust the range as needed
    allocator->populate(numElements);
    return nullptr;
}

void run(CRCIndicationAllocator* allocator, unsigned int numThreads) {
    std::vector<pthread_t> threads;
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < numThreads; ++i) {
        pthread_t thread;
        pthread_create(&thread, nullptr, worker, allocator);
        threads.push_back(thread);
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        pthread_join(thread, nullptr);
    }

    allocator->deallocate();
}
```

This code creates a `CRCIndicationAllocator` class that manages the memory allocation and population of the structures. The `populate()` function takes care of dynamically allocating memory when needed, and it populates the structures with random data. It also logs the operation details, including the number of elements allocated and populated, and the time taken for the operation.

The `run()` function creates a specified number of worker threads that call the `worker()` function to perform the actual work. The worker function simply calls the `populate()` method on the provided allocator instance.

This code is designed to be modular, as you can replace the logging mechanism by changing the implementation of the `log_message()` function. It also handles potential external data source failures gracefully by checking for memory allocation success and logging errors when they occur. The code is thread-safe due to the use of a mutex for protecting the data vector during memory allocation and population operations.']

[" Here's a basic implementation of the `generatePEMCertificate` function using Node.js and the `axios` library for HTTP requests. This example demonstrates some of the required functionality but may need further refinement to meet all your specific requirements.

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const fs = require('fs');
const Stream = require('stream');

class ConcurrentStream extends Stream.Transform {
  constructor(concurrency) {
    super({ objectMode: true });
    this._concurrency = concurrency;
    this._queue = [];
  }

  _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
    if (this._queue.length < this._concurrency) {
      this._queue.push(chunk);
      this.push(chunk);
    } else {
      this._queue.push(chunk);
    }
    callback();
  }

  async _flush(callback) {
    for (const chunk of this._queue) {
      await new Promise((resolve) => {
        this.push(chunk, () => {
          resolve();
        });
      });
    }
    callback();
  }
}

async function generatePEMCertificate({ privateKeyUrl, certificateUrl, logLevel = 'info', logDestination }) {
  const logger = require('winston')[logLevel];
  const concurrency = 4; // Adjust this value based on your needs

  const keyStream = axios.create().dataStream({ url: privateKeyUrl });
  const certStream = axios.create().dataStream({ url: certificateUrl });

  const concatStream = new ConcurrentStream(concurrency);
  concatStream.on('data', (chunk) => {
    fs.appendFileSync(`certificate.pem`, chunk.toString());
  });

  keyStream.pipe(concatStream);
  certStream.pipe(concatStream);

  await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    concatStream.on('end', resolve);
    concatStream.on('error', reject);
  });

  if (logLevel >= 'debug') {
    logger.debug(`Generated certificate saved to certificate.pem`);
  }
}
```

This example demonstrates a function that fetches private key and certificate data concurrently using the `axios` library's dataStream method, which allows for streaming data from remote sources. The data is then concatenated into a single file (`certificate.pem`) using a custom stream class called `ConcurrentStream`.

The function also includes support for logging with Winston and configuration options for setting the log level and destination. Error handling is minimal in this example, but you can extend it to cover edge cases such as missing keys or certificates, incorrect file formats, and timeouts during data collection.

To test the function, you can use mock OAuth requests and remote endpoints by replacing `privateKeyUrl` and `certificateUrl` with appropriate values."]

