[' Here's a Java implementation that follows your specifications, using the ObjectInputStream for deserialization and the Strategy pattern for handling diverse class types. The code includes checks for input size, checksum validation, and logging.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Deserializer {
    private static final int MAX_INPUT_SIZE = 1048576; // 1MB
    private static final String LOGGER_NAME = "Deserializer";

    private Map<String, DeserializationStrategy> strategies;

    public Deserializer(Map<String, Class<?>> classTypes) {
        this.strategies = new HashMap<>();
        for (Map.Entry<String, Class<?>> entry : classTypes.entrySet()) {
            strategies.put(entry.getKey(), new DeserializationStrategy(entry.getValue()));
        }
    }

    public <T> T deserialize(byte[] data) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException, NoSuchAlgorithmException, InstantiationException, IllegalAccessException, InvocationTargetException {
        if (data.length > MAX_INPUT_SIZE) {
            System.out.println("Warning: Input size exceeds 1MB. Truncating array.");
            data = Arrays.copyOf(data, MAX_INPUT_SIZE);
        }

        ByteArrayInputStream inputStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
        ObjectInputStream objectInputStream = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);

        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        byte[] checksum = md.digest(data);

        T result;
        try {
            result = (T) strategies.get(new String(checksum, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)).deserialize(objectInputStream);
        } finally {
            objectInputStream.close();
        }

        if (!Arrays.equals(checksum, calculateChecksum(result))) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Deserialized object checksum does not match expected value.");
        }

        return result;
    }

    private byte[] calculateChecksum(Object obj) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        ByteArrayOutputStream outputStream = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        ObjectOutput objectOutput = new ObjectOutputStream(outputStream);
        objectOutput.writeObject(obj);
        objectOutput.flush();
        return objectOutput.toByteArray();
    }
}

interface DeserializationStrategy {
    <T> T deserialize(ObjectInputStream inputStream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException;
}
```

In this example, the `Deserializer` class takes a map of class types as a constructor argument and uses it to create instances of `DeserializationStrategy` for each supported class. The `deserialize()` method handles deserialization, checksum validation, input size checks, and logging.

The `DeserializationStrategy` interface is used to separate the deserialization logic for different classes, allowing for easy extension and customization as needed.

To use this code, you can create a new instance of `Deserializer` with a map containing the supported class types:

```java
Map<String, Class<?>> classTypes = new HashMap<>();
classTypes.put("Class1", Class1.class);
classTypes.put("Class2", Class2.class);
// Add more classes as needed
Deserializer deserializer = new Deserializer(classTypes);
```

You can then use the `deserialize()` method to process serialized data:

```java
byte[] serializedData = ...; // Load serialized data from some source
try {
    MyClassType deserializedObject = deserializer.deserialize(serializedData);
    // Process the deserialized object
} catch (Exception e) {
    // Handle exceptions
}
```']

