    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        threads.push_back(std::thread([&]() { allocateNetworkEvents(rand() % 1000, [](auto events) { /* Handle the allocated events */ }); }));
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) thread.join();

    log("Total successful allocations: " + std::to_string(successfulAllocations));
    log("Total reconstruction attempts: " + std::to_string(reconstructionAttempts));

    return 0;
}
```

This implementation uses a simple heuristic for event reconstruction, which assumes that gaps in sequence numbers are due to missing events. It fills the gap by copying the last known valid event and incrementing its sequence number. The heuristic may not always be accurate, but it is efficient and can handle large values of `N`.

The logging framework is left as a placeholder for you to implement your preferred logging solution. The function also uses std::unique_lock to ensure thread safety when modifying shared data structures like historicalData and the reconstructionAttempts and successfulAllocations counters.']

